---
/**
 * The first screen. The picture, then one claim, one qualifier, one action.
 *
 * The banner used to sit UNDER the headline, the lede, the buttons and the
 * proof line — which put it below the fold on every laptop, measured at
 * 1440x900 and 1280x800. It is now the first element in the document body
 * after the nav, full-bleed, and the headline follows it. That order is the
 * whole point: the art is the argument this page opens with, and an argument
 * you have to scroll for is not the one you made.
 *
 * The band is height-constrained rather than free-flowing, so that the
 * headline, the lede, the buttons AND the proof line still land inside the
 * first screen at 800px of viewport height. Trading the whole hero for the
 * image would have swapped one thing below the fold for four.
 *
 * The headline is the README's own `<h3 align="center">`, word for word, and
 * the lede is compressed from the paragraph under it. Nothing on this page is
 * allowed to promise more than the README does, and the fastest way to keep
 * that true is to quote it.
 */
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import banner from '../../../../assets/banner.jpg';
import { DOCS, REPO, RELEASE } from './urls';
---

<section class="hero" id="top">
  {/*
    The one image on the page. `Picture` emits AVIF and WebP with the JPEG as
    the fallback and a `sizes` hint, because the source is a 1600x872 JPEG of
    477 kB and shipping that untouched would cost more than everything else on
    this page put together.

    `loading="eager"` with `fetchpriority="high"`: it is now the FIRST element
    on the page as well as the largest, so lazy-loading it would delay the
    Largest Contentful Paint it is.

    This band is deliberately NOT wrapped in `.reveal`. Everything else here
    fades in on scroll; the thing the reader is supposed to see before they do
    anything at all must not be waiting on an observer.
  */}
  <div class="hero__banner">
    <Picture
      src={banner}
      formats={['avif', 'webp']}
      fallbackFormat="jpg"
      widths={[640, 1024, 1600]}
      sizes="100vw"
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
      alt="A jackal-headed conductor standing before a hall of agent workstations, each screen showing its own status: code optimization at 65 percent, a critical logic failure, data gathering stalled, self-improvement required."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="l-wrap hero__body">
    <div class="hero__badges reveal">
      <span class="badge badge--gold badge--dot">v2 &mdash; under construction, in public</span>
      <span class="badge">Claude Code plugin</span>
      <span class="badge">Apache-2.0</span>
    </div>

    <h1 class="reveal" style="--l-delay: 60ms">
      The more you use it, <em>the better it gets.</em>
    </h1>

    <p class="hero__lede reveal" style="--l-delay: 140ms">
      Tyran is a task conductor for Claude Code. It interviews you, plans, and drives a team of
      fresh-context agents through your work &mdash; and <strong
        >refuses to believe any agent that can&rsquo;t show raw command output as proof</strong
      >. Every closed initiative ends in a retrospective that improves Tyran itself, against your
      repo. Never your product code.
    </p>

    <div class="hero__cta reveal" style="--l-delay: 220ms">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="#install">Install it &mdash; two commands</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="#compounding">See what compounds</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={DOCS.gettingStarted}>Docs</a>
    </div>

    <p class="hero__proof reveal" style="--l-delay: 300ms">
      <span>0 runtime dependencies</span>
      <span>867 tests green at v0.1.0</span>
      <span>never writes into your ~/.claude</span>
      <span
        ><a href={REPO} rel="noopener">GitHub</a> &middot; <a href={RELEASE} rel="noopener"
          >v0.1.0</a
        ></span
      >
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
